│ patch_name               │ beam-search-greedy-hybrid                                                                 │
│ patch_description        │ The current approach uses a single greedy pass with random sampling, which is fragile —   │
│                          │ one bad early choice can lock in a poor schedule. A better approach is (1) run many       │
│                          │ randomized greedy restarts and keep the best schedule found, and (2) use beam search:     │
│                          │ maintain several partial schedules (the beam), and at each step expand each beam          │
│                          │ candidate by evaluating all remaining transactions with the actual cost function          │
│                          │ `get_opt_seq_cost`, keeping the top-k cheapest partial schedules. This explores a much    │
│                          │ larger portion of the schedule space while still relying on the true makespan cost rather │
│                          │ than heuristics like transaction length. To keep runtime reasonable, the beam width and   │
│                          │ number of restarts are moderate, and evaluating full cost per candidate is the dominant   │
│                          │ cost, so we cap beam width based on workload size. Since it's a minimization problem      │
│                          │ where cost is monotone in added transactions, pruning by partial makespan is sound.       │
│ num_applied              │ 1                                                                                         │
