│ patch_type               │ full                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ conflict_beam_grasp                                                                       │
│ patch_description        │ The original uses single-path greedy with random sampling (GRASP-like) but evaluates      │
│                          │ full-sequence costs and commits to one path, easily falling into local optima. I propose  │
│                          │ a hybrid: (1) a beam search that maintains multiple promising partial schedules,          │
│                          │ selecting each next transaction by actual incremental makespan cost (via                  │
│                          │ workload.get_opt_seq_cost on partial sequences), pruning to a beam width; (2) GRASP-style │
│                          │ randomized restarts with greedy-random candidate pools (choose among top-k near-min       │
│                          │ candidates) to diversify; (3) a final improvement pass doing adjacent-swap local search   │
│                          │ on the best schedule found, since swapping adjacent conflicting transactions can directly │
│                          │ reduce conflict delay without full re-evaluation. Evaluating true makespan of partial     │
│                          │ sequences at each step grounds the heuristic in actual objective rather than proxies like │
│                          │ txn length.                                                                               │
