│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ insertion_moves_local_search                                                              │
│ patch_description        │ The current local search only swaps nearby pairs (window of 5, 3 rounds). Scheduling      │
│                          │ research shows that for sequencing problems, "insertion" moves (remove a transaction and  │
│                          │ re-insert it at another position) often find improvements that swaps miss, especially     │
│                          │ when a long transaction should move far back. I propose: (1) add an insertion-move pass   │
│                          │ to the local search, alternating with the swap pass; (2) widen the swap window slightly;  │
│                          │ (3) increase restarts so the randomized greedy produces more diverse starting points.     │
│                          │ Each candidate is evaluated with the actual makespan function `get_opt_seq_cost`, so      │
│                          │ improvements are guaranteed to be real makespan reductions. This should strictly lower    │
│                          │ makespan relative to the current swap-only search since every existing move is still      │
│                          │ available and new improving moves can be accepted.                                        │
│ num_applied              │ 2                                                                                         │
│ api_costs                │ $0.0062                                                                                   │
