│ patch_name               │ insertion_plus_swap_local_search                                                          │
│ patch_description        │ The best prior program (score 3367) used GRASP construction plus adjacent-swap local      │
│                          │ search. The recommendation says insertion moves beat swaps, but they explore different    │
│                          │ neighborhoods. My plan: build schedules with GRASP (greedy randomized construction        │
│                          │ picking among top-k cheapest next transactions using true incremental makespan), then     │
│                          │ improve each with a hybrid local search that alternates full insertion sweeps (move a     │
│                          │ transaction to any other position) with adjacent-swap sweeps, both evaluated on true      │
│                          │ makespan. Keep multiple restarts and return the best overall. Insertion moves can fix     │
│                          │ ordering mistakes that adjacent swaps cannot (e.g., moving a heavily-conflicting          │
│                          │ transaction earlier/later by many positions), while swaps unlock exchanges insertion      │
│                          │ can't reach cheaply. Evaluating on the true objective (get_opt_seq_cost) avoids heuristic │
│                          │ misguidance.                                                                              │
