│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ annealed_insertion_search                                                                 │
│ patch_description        │ The current program builds a greedy randomized solution and then applies strict-descent   │
│                          │ swap/insertion local search, which gets trapped in local optima. To escape these local    │
│                          │ optima, I add simulated-annealing-style acceptance on the insertion neighborhood: each    │
│                          │ round, we try a random insertion move (remove a transaction from position i and reinsert  │
│                          │ at position j) and accept it if it improves cost OR with a small probability if it is     │
│                          │ only slightly worse (within a temperature-controlled threshold). The temperature decays   │
│                          │ over the rounds. We track and return the best-so-far schedule, never letting an accepted  │
│                          │ worsening move destroy the best solution found. This extends the proven insertion         │
│                          │ neighborhood (which achieved makespan 292) with a stronger metaheuristic search strategy, │
│                          │ typically yielding better makespans on such sequencing problems. I keep the greedy        │
│                          │ restarts for diversification and reduce the pure best-improvement descent to a quick      │
│                          │ warm-start, spending the remaining budget on annealed moves evaluated with the true       │
│                          │ makespan cost function.                                                                   │
