│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ greedy_restarts_local_search                                                              │
│ patch_description        │ The current beam search is expensive (it evaluates O(n·beam_width) full-sequence costs    │
│                          │ per level, which is very slow for n=100) yet scored worse (301) than the earlier program  │
│                          │ that combined randomized greedy restarts with a swap+reinsertion local search (292).      │
│                          │ Step-by-step reasoning:                                                                   │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 1. **Restart-based randomized greedy is a strong constructive heuristic**: each restart   │
│                          │ uses the *true* makespan (via `get_opt_seq_cost`) to pick the next transaction that       │
│                          │ increases makespan the least, sampling a few candidates per step. Multiple random starts  │
│                          │ diversify the search over orderings since conflict structure makes the cost landscape     │
│                          │ rugged.                                                                                   │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 2. **Local search refines constructive solutions**: pairwise swaps and single-transaction │
│                          │ reinsertion (a form of "or-opt" move) fix locally suboptimal orderings that greedy        │
│                          │ commits to. This directly targets makespan, unlike static heuristics (length, write       │
│                          │ count).                                                                                   │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 3. **Budget management**: Instead of beam search (slow, low quality here), we allocate    │
│                          │ the time to many greedy restarts followed by local search only on the best candidates. We │
│                          │ cap the number of improvement rounds to keep runtime bounded.                             │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 4. **Keep best-so-far across everything**: we track the global best schedule found by any │
│                          │ restart or local search, guaranteeing we never return something worse than the greedy     │
│                          │ best.                                                                                     │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ This should match or beat the 292 makespan of the second prior program since it uses the  │
│                          │ same core (greedy restarts + swap/reinsertion local search) while being the               │
│                          │ proven-best-performing structure among the evaluated variants.                            │
