│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ first_improvement_shuffled_restarts                                                       │
│ patch_description        │ The recommendation analysis shows the current local search uses best-improvement          │
│                          │ relocation with full double loop over all (i, j) pairs, which is expensive per round.     │
│                          │ Switching to first-improvement with a shuffled i-order lets us accept the first improving │
│                          │ move immediately and break the inner loop, dramatically cutting per-round query count.    │
│                          │ The saved time budget lets us raise the restart cap from 20 to 45 within the same         │
│                          │ 100-second deadline. More deep restarts (random greedy + local search polish) explore     │
│                          │ more basins of attraction, which prior generations showed helps. We keep a final          │
│                          │ best-improvement polish round at the end of each restart's local search so results are    │
│                          │ fully refined before being compared to the global best.                                   │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Step-by-step: (1) In `local_search_insertion`, pre-shuffle the index order each round and │
│                          │ iterate in that randomized order. (2) When an improving relocation is found for position  │
│                          │ i, accept it immediately and break out of the j loop (first-improvement), continuing with │
│                          │ the next shuffled i. (3) Run this until no improvement, then do one final                 │
│                          │ best-improvement pass to polish. (4) Raise restart cap to 45 since each restart is now    │
│                          │ much cheaper, giving many more independent search trajectories within the same 100-second │
│                          │ budget, which should lower the best makespan found.                                       │
