│ patch_description        │ The best prior program (3401) used pure-greedy construction followed by an annealing      │
│                          │ insertion local search. The current program's ILS regressed (3247) likely because its     │
│                          │ limited insertion neighborhoods get stuck. Key parameters to tune: greedy exploration     │
│                          │ rate and sample count during construction. I implement a multi-restart construction where │
│                          │ the greedy sample rate decays from 0.95 to 0.85 across restarts (`sample_rate = 0.95 -    │
│                          │ 0.10*(restart/max_restarts)`) and `num_samples` grows from 8 to 12, feeding each greedy   │
│                          │ solution into the proven annealing insertion descent. Early restarts exploit greedy       │
│                          │ signal; later restarts diversify. A cost cache avoids recomputation, and the best         │
│                          │ solution across restarts is returned within the time budget. This follows the             │
│                          │ recommendation (pure parameter mutation on the known-optimum region) while restoring the  │
│                          │ stronger local search structure.                                                          │
